"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. Preoperative PSA was elevated at 8.5. The surgical findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), pT2c stage with no extraprostatic extension, positive surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. The absence of removed lymph nodes, noted as pNx, widens uncertainty regarding nodal status.\n\nCountervailing findings include the absence of lymphovascular invasion and seminal vesicle invasion. The discrepancy between the single biopsy and the prostatectomy specimen regarding Gleason pattern and ISUP grade introduces uncertainty. The missing previous notes prevent a complete assessment of prior treatment and follow-up. Postoperative PSA and its trajectory are critical unknowns that would significantly revise this prognosis. Missing nodal sampling further complicates the assessment of regional disease control. The fixed estimated horizon is 46.50 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 27.90–65.10 months; its coverage has not been validated."
